---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import EmailCapture from '../components/EmailCapture.astro';
// Affiliate links: placeholders marked TODO until accounts approved.
---
<Base
  title="Files Lost? Here's the Recovery Route | Save My Gig!"
  description="Your USB couldn't be rescued on-site. Your files may still be recoverable, here's exactly what to do, and what not to do."
  path="/files-lost"
  noindex
>
  <main class="wrap">
    <div class="status-line red">Recovery_Mode</div>

    <h1 style="margin-bottom:1rem">The night was rough.<br /><span class="accent">The files may still be alive.</span></h1>
    <p class="dim" style="margin-bottom:2rem">"Not readable" almost never means "gone". Most failed USBs still hold the data, the drive just can't present it anymore. What you do next decides whether it's recoverable.</p>

    <div class="panel panel-danger">
      <h3 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem">⚠️ First: stop using the drive</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Do not format it.</strong> Formatting destroys the recovery chances.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not keep re-plugging it</strong> into players, every mount can make it worse.</li>
        <li>Put it aside until you're at a computer with recovery software.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem">Step 1 - Recovery software (works in most cases)</h3>
      <p class="dim">If the computer detects the drive at all (even without showing files), software can usually pull the music off it. Two tools I'd actually use:</p>
      <!-- TODO: replace with real affiliate links once accounts approved -->
      <a href="/recovery" class="btn btn-go" data-event="affiliate_click" data-program="recovery_page" data-placement="files_lost">
        🛠 See the recovery tools →
      </a>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem">Step 2 - If the drive is physically dead</h3>
      <p class="dim" style="margin-bottom:0">No light, not detected anywhere, or it got hit/wet: software can't reach it. A recovery lab can, they open the drive and read the memory directly. Expensive, so only for irreplaceable files. <a href="/recovery#lab">When to use a lab →</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem">Step 3 - Make this the last time</h3>
      <p class="dim">The DJs this never happens to aren't lucky, they run a system. It takes one evening to set up.</p>
      <a href="/prepare" class="btn btn-neutral" data-event="step_completed" data-step="prepare_click" data-placement="files_lost">
        🛡 Set up the prevention system
      </a>
    </div>

    <EmailCapture
      source="files-lost"
      title="Never have this night again"
      blurb="We will email you the printable Emergency Card and the pre-gig checklist. Next time a drive dies it is a five-minute inconvenience, not a lost set. No spam, unsubscribe in one click."
    />

    <p class="dim" style="text-align:center;margin-top:2rem">
      <a href="/feedback">Tell us what happened →</a>
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
